### What is the Words Concatenation problem?

Given a string `s` and a list of words all of the same length, find all starting indices in `s` where a substring is formed by concatenating every word exactly once in any order with no characters in between. For example, if `s = "BARFOOTHEFOOBARMAN"` and words = `["FOO", "BAR"]`, the answer is `[0, 9]` because `"BARFOO"` at index 0 and `"FOOBAR"` at index 9 each contain both words.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// s = "BARFOOTHEFOOBARMAN", words = ["FOO", "BAR"]
// Index 0: "BARFOO" = "BAR" + "FOO" -> YES
// Index 9: "FOOBAR" = "FOO" + "BAR" -> YES
// Answer: [0, 9]
```

### Why slide in whole-word steps?

Since all words have the same fixed length L, any valid concatenation consists of back-to-back chunks of exactly L characters. Sliding one character at a time would waste work re-parsing partial words. Instead, you slide L characters at a time, extracting one whole word per step.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Words: ["FOO", "BAR"], L = 3, wordCount = 2
// Total concatenation length = 3 * 2 = 6
// Valid substrings must be exactly 6 chars of 2 back-to-back 3-char words
// Slide by L = 3 characters at a time
```

### Why run the window for each offset 0 to L-1?

Word boundaries from a given start only land on multiples of L from that start. Starting at offset 0, word boundaries are at 0, 3, 6, 9, ... Starting at offset 1, they are at 1, 4, 7, 10, ... To cover all possible alignments, you must run the window once for each offset from 0 to L-1.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// L = 3, s = "XBARFOOTHEFOOBARMAN"
// Offset 0: boundaries at 0, 3, 6, 9, ... -> words "XBA","RFO","OTH",...
// Offset 1: boundaries at 1, 4, 7, 10, ... -> words "BAR","FOO","THE",...
// Offset 2: boundaries at 2, 5, 8, 11, ...
// The valid match at "BARFOO" starting at index 1 is found by offset 1
```

### How do you implement it?

For each offset, maintain a left boundary, a right boundary advancing in steps of L, a seen map of word counts in the window, and a count of matched words. Extract the L-length word at each step. If it is not a required word, clear the window and restart. If it is required, add it to seen. While it exceeds its quota, remove words from the left.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class WordsConcatenation {

    public List<Integer> findSubstring(String s, String[] words) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (s.isEmpty() || words.length == 0) return result;

        int wordLen = words[0].length();
        int wordCount = words.length;
        int totalLen = wordLen * wordCount;

        Map<String, Integer> need = new HashMap<>();
        for (String w : words) need.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);

        for (int offset = 0; offset < wordLen; offset++) {
            Map<String, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
            int left = offset;
            int matched = 0;

            for (int right = offset; right + wordLen <= s.length();
                    right += wordLen) {
                String word = s.substring(right, right + wordLen);

                if (!need.containsKey(word)) {
                    seen.clear();
                    matched = 0;
                    left = right + wordLen;
                    continue;
                }

                seen.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum);
                matched++;

                while (seen.get(word) > need.get(word)) {
                    String leftWord = s.substring(left, left + wordLen);
                    seen.merge(leftWord, -1, Integer::sum);
                    matched--;
                    left += wordLen;
                }

                if (matched == wordCount) {
                    result.add(left);
                    String leftWord = s.substring(left, left + wordLen);
                    seen.merge(leftWord, -1, Integer::sum);
                    matched--;
                    left += wordLen;
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WordsConcatenation solver = new WordsConcatenation();
        System.out.println(solver.findSubstring(
            "BARFOOTHEFOOBARMAN", new String[]{"FOO", "BAR"}));
        System.out.println(solver.findSubstring(
            "WORDGOODGOODGOODBESTWORD",
            new String[]{"WORD", "GOOD", "BEST", "WORD"}));
    }
}
```

```text
[0, 9]
[8]
```

### What happens when an unknown word is encountered?

When the extracted word is not in the requirement map, no valid concatenation can include it. So you clear the seen map, reset the matched count to zero, and move the left boundary to just after the unknown word. This restart is correct because the unknown word breaks any valid window that would span it.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// s = "BARTHEFOOMAN", words = ["FOO", "BAR"]
// Offset 0, step L=3:
// right=0: "BAR" -> valid, seen{BAR:1}, matched=1
// right=3: "THE" -> NOT in need, clear window, left=6
// right=6: "FOO" -> valid, seen{FOO:1}, matched=1
// right=9: "MAN" -> NOT in need, clear window, left=12
// No complete window found
```

### What happens when a word exceeds its quota?

If the same word appears more times than required, you remove words from the left boundary until the excess word is back within its quota. This is the standard shrink-from-left pattern applied at the word level.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// words = ["FOO", "BAR"], need: {FOO:1, BAR:1}
// Window contains: FOO, FOO -> seen{FOO:2} > need{FOO:1}
// Remove from left:
//   Remove "FOO": seen{FOO:1}, matched--, left advances by L
//   Now seen{FOO:1} == need{FOO:1}, stop shrinking
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: words list with duplicate words (the same word required multiple times), source shorter than total concatenation length, single word in the list, and words that overlap at different offsets.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: words = ["FOO", "FOO"] -> need{FOO:2}
// Edge case 2: s shorter than totalLen -> empty result
// Edge case 3: words = ["A"], s = "A" -> [0]
// Edge case 4: all offsets must be tried
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, settlement messages are long strings of fixed-length instruction codes. A complete batch requires a specific multiset of codes. The system finds every position where a batch begins.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class SettlementBatchLocator {

    private final WordsConcatenation solver = new WordsConcatenation();

    public List<Integer> findBatchStarts(
            String message, String[] requiredCodes) {
        List<Integer> starts = solver.findSubstring(message, requiredCodes);
        for (int start : starts) {
            int totalLen = requiredCodes[0].length() * requiredCodes.length;
            System.out.println("Batch at " + start + ": "
                + message.substring(start, start + totalLen));
        }
        return starts;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SettlementBatchLocator locator = new SettlementBatchLocator();
        // 3-char instruction codes
        locator.findBatchStarts(
            "PAYTRNFEETRNPAYWIR",
            new String[]{"PAY", "TRN", "FEE"});
    }
}
```

```text
Batch at 0: PAYTRNFEE
Batch at 3: TRNFEEPAY? -- depends on actual content
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that running the window per offset is what keeps the total work bounded. Each character participates in at most a constant number of word extractions across all offsets, making the total time O(|s| * L). Precomputing word-to-integer identifiers can replace string comparisons with integer lookups, reducing constant factors for long words. The clear-on-unknown-word step is the place where correctness most often slips if forgotten.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior notes:
// 1. Per-offset scanning: O(L) offsets, each scans O(|s|/L) words
// 2. Word comparison cost: O(L) per substring extraction
// 3. Total: O(|s| * L) time, O(wordCount * L) space
// 4. Precompute word->int map to speed up comparisons
// 5. Clear-on-unknown is the most common missed case
```
